# Filter grammar (views and list_emails)

```
FilterSpec = {
  match: 'all' | 'any'          // how the top-level conditions and groups combine
  conditions: Condition[]
  groups?: { match: 'all' | 'any'; conditions: Condition[] }[]   // one nesting level
}
Condition = { field, operator, value? }   // value omitted for read_status and has_attachment
```

| field | operators | value | meaning |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `mailbox` | `is`, `is_not` | mailbox id (from list_mailboxes) | The email belongs (is) or does not belong (is_not) to that mailbox. |
| `label` | `contains`, `not_contains` | MANUAL label id (from list_labels, isAiLabel false) | The email has (contains) or does not have (not_contains) that manual label. An AI label id here matches nothing: use ai_label. |
| `ai_label` | `contains`, `not_contains` | AI label id (from list_labels, isAiLabel true) | The email has or does not have that AI label. |
| `keyword_subject` | `contains`, `not_contains` | text | The subject contains (or does not contain) the text, ignoring case. |
| `sender_address` | `equals`, `contains` | email address or fragment | Compares the sender ADDRESS only (not the display name), ignoring case. |
| `sender_domain` | `equals`, `contains` | domain such as acme.com | equals: the sender address ends with @domain (subdomains do not match). contains: the text appears anywhere in the sender address, local part included. |
| `date` | `before`, `after`, `between` | ISO 8601 date-time; for between, "start|end" (both inclusive) | Email date. before and after are strict (the instant itself is excluded); between includes both ends. A bare date such as 2026-09-30 means 00:00 UTC that day, so end a range with T23:59:59.999Z. Invalid dates are rejected. |
| `read_status` | `is_true`, `is_false` | none (omit value) | is_true = read, is_false = unread. |
| `has_attachment` | `is_true`, `is_false` | none (omit value) | The email has attachments (is_true) or not (is_false). |

## Rules
- match all = every condition and group must hold; match any = at least one.
- groups add one level of nesting: each group has its own match and conditions.
- There is no folder field: choose the folder with list_emails folder (views show INBOX by default).
- Only label, ai_label and keyword_subject have a negative operator (not_contains); a sender cannot be excluded with this grammar.
- Label conditions take label ids, never names.
- At most 50 conditions in total, 500 characters per value.
- Queries are stopped after 10 seconds (QUERY_TIMEOUT): simplify the filter if that happens.

## Examples
### Newsletters from two domains
```json
{
  "match": "any",
  "conditions": [
    {
      "field": "sender_domain",
      "operator": "equals",
      "value": "substack.com"
    },
    {
      "field": "sender_domain",
      "operator": "equals",
      "value": "mailchimp.com"
    }
  ]
}
```
### Unread invoices received in September 2026
```json
{
  "match": "all",
  "conditions": [
    {
      "field": "ai_label",
      "operator": "contains",
      "value": "<id of the Invoices AI label>"
    },
    {
      "field": "read_status",
      "operator": "is_false"
    },
    {
      "field": "date",
      "operator": "between",
      "value": "2026-09-01T00:00:00.000Z|2026-09-30T23:59:59.999Z"
    }
  ]
}
```
### Emails with attachments from client-a.com or client-b.com, except newsletters
```json
{
  "match": "all",
  "conditions": [
    {
      "field": "has_attachment",
      "operator": "is_true"
    },
    {
      "field": "keyword_subject",
      "operator": "not_contains",
      "value": "newsletter"
    }
  ],
  "groups": [
    {
      "match": "any",
      "conditions": [
        {
          "field": "sender_domain",
          "operator": "equals",
          "value": "client-a.com"
        },
        {
          "field": "sender_domain",
          "operator": "equals",
          "value": "client-b.com"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```
